\details{
\code{morph_dendrogram()} expects \code{analysis_data} as produced by
\code{\link[=morph_matrix_setting]{morph_matrix_setting()}}: a numeric data frame with \code{id} and \code{taxon}
columns plus one column per morphological trait, with specimen IDs as row
names. The trait blocks (\code{"veg"}, \code{"flo"}, \code{"fru"}, or any subset thereof)
are not passed in separately — they are read directly from the
\code{"base_cols"} attribute that \code{\link[=morph_matrix_setting]{morph_matrix_setting()}} attaches to
\code{analysis_data}. If \code{analysis_data} has no \code{"base_cols"} attribute, the
function stops with an informative error.

The \code{dendro_blocks} argument controls which trait sets are analyzed:

\itemize{
\item \code{"all"}: runs the analysis on each individual block in \code{base_cols}
(typically \code{"veg"}, \code{"flo"}, \code{"fru"}), as well as on every pairwise and
full combination of these blocks (\code{"vegflo"}, \code{"vegfru"}, \code{"flofru"},
\code{"vegflofru"}) that can be fully built from the blocks present;
\item a character vector naming one or more blocks in \code{base_cols}: runs
the analysis only on the requested block(s), individually.
}

For each trait block, rows with missing values in the block's columns are
removed via \code{\link[stats:na.omit]{stats::na.omit()}}. Blocks with fewer than 3 remaining
specimens or fewer than 2 traits are skipped with a warning.

When a block has more than 2 traits, the traits used for clustering are
first narrowed to those contributing more than the average expected
contribution to PC1 (\code{100 / p}, where \code{p} is the number of traits in the
block) — the same reference-line criterion used by
\code{\link[factoextra:fviz_contrib]{factoextra::fviz_contrib()}} to flag "significant" variables. If fewer
than 2 traits pass this criterion, the top 2 contributing traits are used
instead, so that at least 2 traits are always retained for clustering.

A distance matrix is computed on the retained traits with
\code{\link[vegan:vegdist]{vegan::vegdist()}} using \code{dist_method}. Each of \code{linkage_candidates} is fit
with \code{\link[stats:hclust]{stats::hclust()}}, and the linkage whose cophenetic distances
correlate best with the original distance matrix (Sokal & Rohlf's
cophenetic correlation) is selected automatically. The number of clusters
\code{k} is then chosen automatically, from 2 up to \code{min(k_max, n - 1)}, as the
value maximizing the average silhouette width (\code{\link[cluster:silhouette]{cluster::silhouette()}}).

For each analyzed block, the function produces:

\itemize{
\item a bar plot of cophenetic correlation by candidate linkage method;
\item a line plot of average silhouette width by candidate \code{k}, with the
selected \code{k} marked;
\item a vertical dendrogram, with branches colored by the statistical
cluster assignment (\code{k} groups) and leaf labels colored by known taxon,
so the two can be visually compared;
\item a horizontal version of the same dendrogram.
}

Diagnostic plots are saved in the formats specified by \code{file_formats}.
Dendrograms are base-R plots and are always saved as both PDF and JPEG,
since \code{file_formats} controls \code{\link[ggplot2:ggplot]{ggplot2::ggplot()}}-based diagnostic output
only.

Leaf labels in each dendrogram are colored by known taxon. These taxon
colours are taken from the \code{"taxon_colors"} attribute attached to
\code{analysis_data} by \code{\link[=morph_matrix_setting]{morph_matrix_setting()}}, so that each taxon keeps the
same colour used in other plots (e.g. \code{\link[=morph_boxplots]{morph_boxplots()}}, \code{\link[=morph_pca]{morph_pca()}}).
If this attribute is missing, or lacks an entry for a taxon present in a
given block, colours are generated automatically from the \code{viridis}
palette for the affected taxa, with a warning. This is independent of
\code{cluster_cols}, which colors the dendrogram \emph{branches} by statistical
cluster rather than by taxon.

